 =?UTF-8?q?ct=20groups=20by=20dragging?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Behaviours:
- In the grouped sidebar (Group threads by project, the boringSidebarEnabled client setting) active threads are ordered by the same manual order as the ungrouped list with the Working section off (sortThreadsForSidebar: keyless threads first by creation or un-settle time, then by activeOrderKey), restricted to each project folder. Ordering by the last send is removed: a newer message no longer moves a card inside its folder. New threads have no order key, so they appear at the top of their folder, as in the ungrouped list.
- A thread card under a project header can be dragged to another position among the cards of its own folder. The dragged card follows the pointer vertically with its indent and the lifted-card look of the ungrouped list; only the cards of the same folder slide aside (dnd-kit's vertical list strategy over that folder's contiguous rows); nothing else moves and no new animation is added.
- The drop is mapped onto the whole active order: the card is placed right before the folder card it was dropped on when it moved up, or right after it when it moved down; threads from other folders, threads no folder shows (collapsed folders, projects without a folder) keep their places. That order goes through the unchanged planSidebarThreadDrop and the same reorderActiveThread commands, optimistic placement and failure toasts as an ungrouped reorder, so the position survives a reload, reaches other devices and shows the same relative order in the ungrouped list.
- A grouped folder reorder writes every order key the plan assigns, including keys for threads that are not rendered (threads in collapsed folders, projects without a folder). The writes resolve their threads from every thread in the Pinned, Active, Working, Snoozed and Settled sections, not from the rendered rows, and the operate-permission check covers every written thread. With keyless active threads [a1, b1, a2], folder A open and folder B collapsed, dragging a2 above a1 stores [a2, a1, b1], and the optimistic drop is released when those writes land, as after an ungrouped reorder. If a planned write has no thread to resolve, the drop does nothing instead of writing part of the order; the ungrouped list never reaches this, because its plans only assign rendered rows.
- A drop on a card of another folder, a pinned, snoozed or settled row, a section marker or nowhere does nothing, and a drop back on the card's own place plans nothing. Project headers are not drop targets. The collision filter rejects such targets while dragging, so no card makes room for them.
- A dragged folder card has a drop target only while the pointer is between the top of its folder's first card and the bottom of its last card. Inside that extent the collision detection ranks only that folder's cards by distance to the dragged card's centre, so a card of a neighbouring folder can never be the nearest centre, a target inside the folder always exists and the folder's first and last positions are reachable. Over a project header, another folder's cards, a shelf or empty space nothing is targeted, no card makes room, and a release does nothing. Rows without a project folder (the ungrouped list) and drags without pointer coordinates keep the unfiltered closest-centre ranking, so the ungrouped list's collision detection is unchanged.
- Every other grouped-mode drag behaves as before: pinned, snoozed and settled rows only drag out as composer context, there is no pin, unpin, settle, un-settle or wake by drag, and the Pinned and Active boundary labels and section hints stay hidden. Dragging a card out of the sidebar onto the composer still mentions it. Grouped folder drags now auto-scroll the sidebar like ungrouped drags.
- The draggable cards are the same as in the ungrouped list (connected environment, loaded server config, not being renamed, no optimistic drop pending, operate permission). The ungrouped list's drag behaviour is unchanged. The web sidebar has no keyboard reorder, so none is added.
- The Snooze trigger and the Discard draft button in a card's action cluster stop propagation of pointerdown, like Mark read / unread and Settle, so a press on them never reaches the row's drag sensor. Neither starts the sweep gesture.
- The user guide says that folders keep the saved active order, that a thread can be dragged within its folder and is saved like an ungrouped reorder, and that it cannot be dragged into another folder or section.

